  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Harbour Light",
    "overview": "Pharos is named after a lighthouse, and its interface works like one. The frame stays still and quiet so that one signal stands out. That signal is a single blue, kept for the places where the developer's attention belongs: the current tab, the one primary action on a screen, and the prompt that says a widget needs setup. Everything else is neutral: system type, pale tonal surfaces, thin fog-colored borders. The platform team's own content and data fill the frame; Pharos itself stays in the background.\n\nThis is an Operate-mode tool, used daily and at a glance, so density stays moderate and the layout is predictable. The aim for components is **soft and approachable**. Developers should feel the app is helping them, not auditing them, even on screens that ask for trust (capability consent, lint errors). Today that shows up as 6px corners, tonal surfaces in place of hard boxes, and gray default buttons. A future refinement may push the softness further.\n\n**This is a provisional baseline.** It accurately records the system as it stands at slice 5, which is still close to the scaffold. Tokens and the rules below hold until they are changed on purpose. These areas are explicitly open: the type scale (there's no display role, and headings use browser-default weights), how far the accent can extend beyond its current uses, a warning/caution color distinct from danger, and a reduced-motion policy.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One accent (Signal Blue). Everything else is neutral or a state color.",
      "Light and dark themes follow `prefers-color-scheme`, defined as token overrides on `:root`.",
      "System UI type. Mono for anything a machine reads: inputs, paths, hosts, code.",
      "Flat surfaces separated by tone and 1px borders. No shadows.",
      "A single radius (6px) on every enclosed shape."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Lamp Rule",
        "body": "Signal Blue appears only where it marks the current place, the next action or a needed setup step. A screen has at most one primary button.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paired Theme Rule",
        "body": "Each color token has a dark-mode value, overridden on `:root` under `prefers-color-scheme: dark`. A new color isn't finished until both values exist.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Machine Text Rule",
        "body": "Text that a developer could paste into a terminal or a config file is set in mono. Prose never is.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tone Not Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Separate a surface with a tonal step or a Fog Line border, never a shadow. If something needs to feel raised, it's an overlay and gets a scrim.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do use the existing tokens (`--accent`, `--surface`, `--border`, `--space-*`, `--radius`) rather than literal values, and define every new color for both themes.",
      "Do keep a single primary (Signal Blue) button per view. Make everything else a default or link button.",
      "Do set paths, hosts, commands, SHAs and inputs in mono (`--mono`).",
      "Do separate surfaces with a tonal step (Open White and Mist) and 1px Fog Line borders.",
      "Do reuse the shared widget states (empty, loading, error, needs setup) rather than designing new ones for each widget."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't add box shadows. Depth is tonal, and overlays get a scrim.",
      "Don't introduce a second accent hue, or use Signal Blue for decoration.",
      "Don't load remote fonts, icon fonts or CDN assets. The strict CSP allows only the app's own scripts and styles.",
      "Don't use browser `prompt()` or `alert()`. Use the in-app Dialog.",
      "Don't let the platform's branding replace the Pharos frame. Platform titles appear as content."
    ]
  }
}